<header><p class="st-kicker">other · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Ravenstein</h1><p class="st-meta">1360 CE – 1800 CE (approx.) · capital Ravenstein</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small lordship on the Maas that stayed outside the Dutch Republic and became a refuge for Catholics in a Protestant land.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ravenstein grew around a castle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Maas</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Brabant</a>. In the 14th century it passed to the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, and from 1360 it was held as a separate lordship by Cleves princes, independent of both Brabant and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-guelders">Guelders</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a> lords of Ravenstein, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-of-cleves-lord-of-ravenstein">Adolf of Cleves</a> and his son Philip, were prominent at the Burgundian court. Later the lordship passed to the dukes of Jülich-Berg and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a> family. When the surrounding lands came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, Ravenstein remained a Catholic enclave, where priests and believers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generality-lands">Generality lands</a> could worship freely.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The lordship was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a> around 1800, ending its separate status. It later became part of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-brabant">North Brabant</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-ravenstein.jpg" alt="Lordship of Ravenstein" width="320" height="208"><figcaption>Image: Michielverbeek · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ravenstein,_windmolen_de_Nijverheid_RM32362_foto4_2016-04-20_11.18.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1360 CE – 1800 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ravenstein</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adolf of Cleves, Philip of Cleves</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Netherlands</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ravenstein</dd></dl></aside></div>
